1
00:00:29,535 --> 00:00:53,213
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a question a friend raised a few days ago, and it's quite common, that once you start becoming steady in your practice, the basic practice of virtue, sense restraint, and you're doing that for

2
00:00:53,970 --> 00:01:04,175
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, a good year or so, and things are maybe... you've got a lot of energy for practice and contemplation, but then it just quietens out.

3
00:01:06,196 --> 00:01:08,957
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no more, like, questions in a way.

4
00:01:10,238 --> 00:01:11,439
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not necessarily free.

5
00:01:11,579 --> 00:01:12,799
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not free from suffering.

6
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:18,162
[SPEAKER_00]: You suffer, but you're now in a very comfortable zone.

7
00:01:19,222 --> 00:01:19,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

8
00:01:20,043 --> 00:01:20,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

9
00:01:20,615 --> 00:01:22,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, well, you're not breaking any precepts.

10
00:01:22,796 --> 00:01:23,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

11
00:01:23,576 --> 00:01:26,057
[SPEAKER_00]: You're comfortable in center strength, you're calm.

12
00:01:27,278 --> 00:01:37,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, that's basically then the time, the Buddha says in the suttas, when you have to kind of arouse energy for things that are not yet attained.

13
00:01:37,463 --> 00:01:45,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as we said so many times, precepts in themselves will not lead, will not guarantee understanding.

14
00:01:45,967 --> 00:01:48,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Precepts are necessary for understanding, but

15
00:01:50,237 --> 00:01:54,779
[SPEAKER_01]: In themselves, they will not lead to understanding if you don't make the effort to understand what needs to be understood.

16
00:01:56,319 --> 00:02:00,821
[SPEAKER_01]: So, although they are not optional, in themselves, they are not sufficient.

17
00:02:01,101 --> 00:02:10,264
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody has been, you know, keep living according to the precepts, sense restraint and so on, great, but now you actually have to make the further effort.

18
00:02:10,344 --> 00:02:12,025
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, oh, what, what now?

19
00:02:13,725 --> 00:02:14,705
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that doesn't change.

20
00:02:14,926 --> 00:02:18,667
[SPEAKER_01]: The lifestyle of precepts doesn't change, but on top of that, you have to start, you know,

21
00:02:19,543 --> 00:02:27,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Arousing a bit of sense of urgency, reflecting about things you are liable, making a point out of it.

22
00:02:28,590 --> 00:02:36,776
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, maybe, perhaps, if the environment is too familiar, too comfortable, something you're very accustomed to, change it up a bit.

23
00:02:37,116 --> 00:02:46,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Experience fatigue, elements, lack of sleep, and see how much of your steady practice remains, and how much of it sort of starts to

24
00:02:47,629 --> 00:02:48,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Be challenged.

25
00:02:48,350 --> 00:02:54,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can also see that even the person is bothered by... might be bothered by things being now too calm.

26
00:02:54,654 --> 00:03:08,585
[SPEAKER_01]: There's... Well, yes, and it's hard, like, it's hard to... it might be hard for somebody to to kind of bring up that energy and determination to to think about things not yet attained and things you're liable, when nothing's directly sort of pressuring your poking.

27
00:03:08,625 --> 00:03:14,830
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, things have been calm relatively, your environment has been steadied, controlled, you live remotely, nothing coming up.

28
00:03:14,910 --> 00:03:15,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's fine.

29
00:03:15,951 --> 00:03:16,511
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm content.

30
00:03:17,092 --> 00:03:17,232
[SPEAKER_01]: But

31
00:03:17,795 --> 00:03:20,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, like there is more to be done, obviously.

32
00:03:21,477 --> 00:03:40,066
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's that's like when you want to just not necessarily destroy your house and leave, burn it and leave and just, you know, become a homeless person to experience the change, but just go somewhere for a while, somewhere different where you're not in control, where you're like, you know, you might have to put up with such elements,

33
00:03:41,531 --> 00:03:47,775
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when we, like, go to forest, go hiking, go somewhere, stay away in a less comfortable environment.

34
00:03:48,195 --> 00:03:49,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Go visit some family.

35
00:03:51,457 --> 00:03:52,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Some relatives.

36
00:03:52,057 --> 00:03:57,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I suppose, yeah, but I'm still trying to keep it along the, you know, practice of sense restraint.

37
00:03:57,160 --> 00:04:00,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, go to people who will be challenging.

38
00:04:00,662 --> 00:04:03,904
[SPEAKER_01]: That's, like, again, that's only because... only if you cannot

39
00:04:04,708 --> 00:04:13,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Yourself, sustain those reflections, there will be a rising sense of urgency, and to practice towards things you haven't yet attained.

40
00:04:13,776 --> 00:04:18,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you can't, or you just try, and the mind just drifts away, then, okay, yeah, change it up a bit.

41
00:04:19,181 --> 00:04:23,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, or even within the setting that you have, don't eat for a day or two.

42
00:04:25,846 --> 00:04:27,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Get up earlier than you would usually.

43
00:04:29,598 --> 00:04:38,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Just to sort of, you know, prod the lazy body and the mind, experience a bit of discomfort, and then you'll see, okay, it's right there, things that I need to deal with.

44
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:48,081
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not abstract, they're not hidden in some like, you know, distant parts of my reflection that I have to spend days of traveling before I arrive at what I need to do.

45
00:04:48,621 --> 00:04:57,283
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, just a bit of lack of sleep, not enough food, a bit of fatigue, and see how much you remain mindful after all of that.

46
00:04:57,323 --> 00:04:59,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Getting your views challenged?

47
00:05:00,165 --> 00:05:01,946
[SPEAKER_01]: That too, exactly, sure, yeah.

48
00:05:02,346 --> 00:05:04,807
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you need to find people who would challenge us.

49
00:05:04,827 --> 00:05:07,728
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, relatives could, you know, like people who were trying to be argumentative.

50
00:05:07,748 --> 00:05:12,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, have your views challenged in a beneficial sense, not just in argumentative.

51
00:05:12,570 --> 00:05:18,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind you, even then, for somebody, it might be enough to like, hey, okay, yeah, I guess, you know, maybe I'm not clear as I thought I was.

52
00:05:20,917 --> 00:05:30,784
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it won't be that hard to find work to do internally, as long as you know that you need to find work to do.

53
00:05:31,444 --> 00:05:32,785
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start looking, you will find it.

54
00:05:33,366 --> 00:05:36,087
[SPEAKER_01]: If sometimes people don't think they should look, then they won't find it.

55
00:05:39,149 --> 00:05:47,695
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one of the things is, once you become calm like that, and you can't see any work, the hindrances are kind of

56
00:05:49,103 --> 00:06:01,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Already coming up, there's some people would say, when I'm brewing up the hindrances, contemplate things that would make you lustful, so that you would get to deal with the lust.

57
00:06:03,654 --> 00:06:09,598
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, so you would see, okay, now I am lustful, okay, let me understand it now.

58
00:06:10,579 --> 00:06:17,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I can see, like, the idea behind it is beneficial, but

59
00:06:19,782 --> 00:06:22,544
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be overshooting it, overshooting the mark.

60
00:06:22,644 --> 00:06:36,856
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like if somebody doesn't, you know, is not even aware that there is still lust, aversion in their mind and so on, you know, if you tell them in a coarse manner, well, bring up the lust or aversion and that's your work, you could see practical benefit of it.

61
00:06:36,916 --> 00:06:40,959
[SPEAKER_01]: However, you shouldn't really go all the way of bring up the lust.

62
00:06:42,195 --> 00:06:43,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Or bring up the aversion.

63
00:06:44,115 --> 00:06:46,116
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is just think about that.

64
00:06:46,396 --> 00:06:49,857
[SPEAKER_01]: If I think about such things, lust will arise and stop there.

65
00:06:49,937 --> 00:06:50,437
[SPEAKER_01]: That's enough.

66
00:06:51,077 --> 00:06:57,998
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to actually excite yourself or wind yourself up in order to do it.

67
00:06:58,698 --> 00:07:05,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's a prospect of being able to do it and that prospect will become apparent as soon as you start thinking about doing it.

68
00:07:07,082 --> 00:07:14,689
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start thinking about winding yourself up, you are already... if there is a possibility of you to be wound up, you are already being wound up just by that possibility.

69
00:07:15,109 --> 00:07:32,685
[SPEAKER_01]: So you need to start recognizing that, because it will be much less efficient and much... you would waste much more effort than if you actually have to fully excite yourself towards sensual desire and then deal with it, or fully kind of wind yourself up with anger and aversion and then deal with it.

70
00:07:34,392 --> 00:07:39,794
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no need to go all the way in, even on the level of the mind.

71
00:07:40,434 --> 00:07:48,477
[SPEAKER_01]: But yes, if you are complacent, if you don't know what you're supposed to do, yeah, just, well, can lust arise in my mind?

72
00:07:49,117 --> 00:07:51,338
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know already, you will have an answer.

73
00:07:51,758 --> 00:07:55,339
[SPEAKER_01]: If I were to think such and such things, I will become lustful.

74
00:07:55,379 --> 00:07:55,699
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

75
00:07:56,100 --> 00:07:59,381
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to experience the lust to recognize that the lust is there.

76
00:07:59,981 --> 00:08:03,002
[SPEAKER_01]: And same, if I recall things that were upsetting for me,

77
00:08:04,673 --> 00:08:08,657
[SPEAKER_01]: And you start a little bit before and you say, okay, I will get upset.

78
00:08:08,878 --> 00:08:09,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, stop there.

79
00:08:09,558 --> 00:08:10,139
[SPEAKER_01]: That's enough.

80
00:08:10,259 --> 00:08:11,620
[SPEAKER_01]: That recognition is sufficient.

81
00:08:12,842 --> 00:08:16,085
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why it's important to qualify where the line is.

82
00:08:16,686 --> 00:08:25,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't qualify, somebody can think that they must go all the way up until mind is wound up with anger in order to deal with it.

83
00:08:25,996 --> 00:08:26,756
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's too far.

84
00:08:27,553 --> 00:08:30,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you will not deal with it up until anger subsides first.

85
00:08:30,616 --> 00:08:31,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can deal with it.

86
00:08:31,897 --> 00:08:39,623
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you know when to stop, such as, yes, think about something that makes you angry, and as you recognize that you're going to get angry if you continue thinking, stop.

87
00:08:41,064 --> 00:08:46,289
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you shouldn't, like, arise hindrances, but you should definitely contemplate about liability to hindrances.

88
00:08:48,450 --> 00:08:53,695
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can say, well, I'm liable to getting angry, I'm liable to suffering on account of some loss.

89
00:08:54,063 --> 00:08:54,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

90
00:08:54,463 --> 00:08:56,685
[SPEAKER_01]: And take this specific example, then.

91
00:08:57,005 --> 00:08:57,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

92
00:08:57,585 --> 00:08:58,586
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I lose somebody.

93
00:08:59,386 --> 00:09:03,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you haven't lost, but start thinking about losing somebody, immediately.

94
00:09:03,849 --> 00:09:18,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you start going that direction, if you are liable, you're gonna start feeling it, and you don't have to now, okay, but I must go through all this whole kind of process of winding myself up, building up all this passion and aversion to deal with it.

95
00:09:18,658 --> 00:09:19,039
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

96
00:09:19,439 --> 00:09:21,200
[SPEAKER_01]: You are already sufficiently wound up.

97
00:09:23,592 --> 00:09:31,875
[SPEAKER_01]: But so, on one hand, it's important to take something specific that could prompt lust, or aversion, or fear, or anxiety in you.

98
00:09:32,315 --> 00:09:39,858
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why it's important, actually, to have something specific is so you don't remain in that, like, abstract plane of, yes, I am liable to loss.

99
00:09:40,918 --> 00:09:42,139
[SPEAKER_01]: What is loss?

100
00:09:42,239 --> 00:09:50,222
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, some people can operate in those categories mentally, but practically speaking, no, no, find something that would affect you if you lose it.

101
00:09:51,026 --> 00:09:56,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about it up until you start recognizing, okay, I'm gonna get wound up and that's when you practice.

102
00:09:56,452 --> 00:09:59,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so now I'll stop and this is my work.

103
00:10:00,015 --> 00:10:03,339
[SPEAKER_01]: The possibility of being wound up means I am already wound up.

104
00:10:04,351 --> 00:10:07,833
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what needs to be endured and seen on its own for what it is.

105
00:10:08,113 --> 00:10:11,315
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you see the danger you're in, the possibilities of... Yeah.

106
00:10:11,555 --> 00:10:11,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

107
00:10:13,076 --> 00:10:14,677
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you have to question yourself.

108
00:10:14,717 --> 00:10:19,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I... Why would I suffer if such... if I... such and such happened?

109
00:10:19,439 --> 00:10:20,180
[SPEAKER_00]: I lose my health.

110
00:10:20,600 --> 00:10:20,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

111
00:10:20,920 --> 00:10:23,402
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you can imagine something.

112
00:10:23,422 --> 00:10:23,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

113
00:10:23,962 --> 00:10:25,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Losing your teeth or something.

114
00:10:25,403 --> 00:10:26,964
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure.

115
00:10:27,284 --> 00:10:30,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can... you can... you can explain why you would suffer physically.

116
00:10:31,838 --> 00:10:35,400
[SPEAKER_01]: But why would you mentally be distraught on account of such an idea?

117
00:10:36,301 --> 00:10:49,489
[SPEAKER_01]: And another reason why it's important to not go all the way, and become completely wound up, or lustful, or angry, is because then everything you do afterwards will be to get rid of the lust or anger, not to understand it, to get rid of the pain of it.

118
00:10:49,989 --> 00:10:59,055
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you stop short of being wound up, face the direction of possibility of being wound up, think about specific things that could wind you up, but before you get wound up, you stop.

119
00:10:59,635 --> 00:11:09,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, thinking about it will not be as rooted in trying to manage the pain and get rid of it, but it will actually be on the level of, okay, so I'm not quite wound up, but I could be wound up.

120
00:11:10,459 --> 00:11:12,539
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the middle ground where practice applies.

121
00:11:12,560 --> 00:11:16,421
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I am liable to suffering, as the Buddha would say.

122
00:11:18,361 --> 00:11:19,142
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to ask, why?

123
00:11:19,382 --> 00:11:19,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I?

124
00:11:19,782 --> 00:11:22,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you're now on that level, then exactly.

125
00:11:22,023 --> 00:11:22,483
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why

126
00:11:24,940 --> 00:11:25,800
[SPEAKER_00]: Why does that exist?

127
00:11:26,160 --> 00:11:27,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, why would I be affected?

128
00:11:27,881 --> 00:11:28,981
[SPEAKER_01]: You can start asking that.

129
00:11:29,101 --> 00:11:32,301
[SPEAKER_01]: But really, it's like, why would I be bothered if I'm affected?

130
00:11:32,501 --> 00:11:34,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that not acceptable to me?

131
00:11:35,242 --> 00:11:41,623
[SPEAKER_01]: If this occurs, and there is the emotional response to it, why is that not... why am I resisting the idea of that?

132
00:11:41,703 --> 00:11:43,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I defending myself against it?

133
00:11:43,984 --> 00:11:44,364
[SPEAKER_01]: And so on.

134
00:11:45,244 --> 00:11:48,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, right now, that possibility is uncomfortable.

135
00:11:48,724 --> 00:11:49,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

136
00:11:49,645 --> 00:11:51,745
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I then... can I then not

137
00:11:53,188 --> 00:12:02,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I not seek discomfort intentionally, but can I also not intentionally and habitually and automatically deny it and resist it and try to get rid of it?

138
00:12:03,617 --> 00:12:08,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I just endure discomfort on that level of could be wound up but not wound up?

139
00:12:09,503 --> 00:12:17,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I just stay there and endure it on that level without being wound up and then making all the effort to manage it and get rid of it and think I dealt with it?

140
00:12:18,183 --> 00:12:22,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I just endure it on this level and learn how to stop resisting internally?

141
00:12:23,544 --> 00:12:27,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's how it weighs on you, through your resistance of it.

142
00:12:27,805 --> 00:12:30,366
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop resisting it, it's just the thing that endures there.

143
00:12:32,967 --> 00:12:39,028
[SPEAKER_01]: But that endurance, as I say so many times, needs to be endured on the right level, which is the level of that...

144
00:12:40,368 --> 00:12:47,010
[SPEAKER_01]: I am about to be wound up, or I could be wound up and experience aversion, lust and so on, but I'm not quite, so I'm stopping.

145
00:12:47,343 --> 00:12:56,109
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, I'm restraining my thinking and, you know, senses and not engaging further, but at the same time, I'm not denying the direction of, you know, where my mind's heading.

146
00:12:57,010 --> 00:13:03,134
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can I induce things on that level, on that more ambiguous level, on that level that's much easier to doubt and overwhelm you?

147
00:13:05,016 --> 00:13:06,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're...

148
00:13:07,225 --> 00:13:11,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Not lustful, then you're lustful, then you apply the practice to get rid of the lust.

149
00:13:12,228 --> 00:13:12,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Done.

150
00:13:12,668 --> 00:13:13,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Clear cut.

151
00:13:13,309 --> 00:13:13,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Simple.

152
00:13:13,829 --> 00:13:17,931
[SPEAKER_01]: A, B, C. You're not angry, then you're angry, then you do this when you're angry.

153
00:13:18,091 --> 00:13:18,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Simple.

154
00:13:18,572 --> 00:13:21,273
[SPEAKER_01]: A, B, C. But none of that is where the dhamma is.

155
00:13:25,075 --> 00:13:29,538
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to be managing those states of mind, because they're painful.

156
00:13:30,778 --> 00:13:35,001
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be not resisting their pain, so that they don't affect you to begin with.

157
00:13:42,528 --> 00:13:46,290
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the possibility that I could suffer is suffering.

158
00:13:46,310 --> 00:13:46,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

159
00:13:47,450 --> 00:13:48,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, it's felt.

160
00:13:48,131 --> 00:13:49,431
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already uncomfortable.

161
00:13:49,491 --> 00:13:52,433
[SPEAKER_01]: If you, if you discern it, that will be felt uncomfortably.

162
00:13:53,413 --> 00:13:56,035
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now, yeah, so now I don't have to go wind myself up.

163
00:13:56,135 --> 00:13:56,455
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

164
00:13:56,515 --> 00:13:57,175
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already there.

165
00:13:57,495 --> 00:13:58,716
[SPEAKER_01]: But I need something more palpable.

166
00:13:58,776 --> 00:13:59,616
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's your problem.

167
00:13:59,636 --> 00:14:04,059
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the lack of discernment and lack of mind development, when you think you need something more palpable.

168
00:14:04,719 --> 00:14:08,761
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only reason you need something more palpable is because you keep seeking something more palpable.

169
00:14:09,292 --> 00:14:11,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, what is palpable is right in front of you.

170
00:14:11,413 --> 00:14:13,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because this is palpable enough.

171
00:14:13,534 --> 00:14:14,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Sufficiently palpable.

172
00:14:14,575 --> 00:14:16,315
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize it, you discern it, you see it.

173
00:14:17,096 --> 00:14:20,037
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, that ambiguity in itself, it's uncomfortable.

174
00:14:21,038 --> 00:14:29,582
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by seeking something more palpable, what you're in reality doing is trying to get rid of discomfort of something more doubtful, less palpable, less clear, less definable.

175
00:14:30,662 --> 00:14:31,903
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already a form of craving.

176
00:14:35,934 --> 00:14:41,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I said even like, you know, ambiguous state of mind, state of mind affected with doubt, for example, it's real as such.

177
00:14:44,139 --> 00:14:46,861
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not clear, lack of clarity, confusion, this or this.

178
00:14:47,401 --> 00:14:51,444
[SPEAKER_01]: That experience as a whole is real as such and that cannot be doubted.

179
00:14:52,024 --> 00:14:58,328
[SPEAKER_01]: But you need to endure it sufficiently on that level of that doubt and ambiguity to recognize it on its own.

180
00:14:59,149 --> 00:15:03,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now, whenever such experience manifests, when you are sort of experiencing it,

181
00:15:05,415 --> 00:15:17,367
[SPEAKER_01]: The habitual action is go towards definition, clarity, answers, not because you recognize the value of the answer, but because through answering or through seeking the answer, you get rid of the pain of the discomfort of the doubt.

182
00:15:18,508 --> 00:15:23,192
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm comfortable in my practice, but I want to practice.

183
00:15:23,473 --> 00:15:24,354
[SPEAKER_00]: I do want to practice.

184
00:15:24,714 --> 00:15:24,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

185
00:15:25,535 --> 00:15:25,975
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let me...

186
00:15:26,436 --> 00:15:26,876
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you do?

187
00:15:27,116 --> 00:15:27,457
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you do?

188
00:15:27,477 --> 00:15:28,257
[SPEAKER_00]: I go and seek practice.

189
00:15:28,438 --> 00:15:28,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

190
00:15:29,279 --> 00:15:29,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

191
00:15:29,819 --> 00:15:36,605
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I watch I wind myself up or try to study or get some get some thoughts going.

192
00:15:36,625 --> 00:15:36,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

193
00:15:37,526 --> 00:15:41,268
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's all because I'm actually stepping away from practice.

194
00:15:41,308 --> 00:15:53,518
[SPEAKER_01]: But I think I said in other talks, like if sometimes, you know, a person reaches that a bit of a kind of status quo, like, oh, it's all right, but I don't see any further what to do.

195
00:15:54,399 --> 00:15:55,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Just tighten your sensory strength.

196
00:15:57,052 --> 00:16:04,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Just, okay, start saying no a bit more, to more things, and you'll see immediately the mind, very sure, very sure.

197
00:16:04,634 --> 00:16:11,977
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't be before long, mind starts, you know, coming up with irrational things, and mind states, and moods, and so on, and there's your practice.

198
00:16:11,997 --> 00:16:12,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Now deal with that.

199
00:16:15,730 --> 00:16:16,611
[SPEAKER_01]: Go and stay in the forest.

200
00:16:16,651 --> 00:16:17,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Go hike.

201
00:16:17,152 --> 00:16:17,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Skip a meal.

202
00:16:17,953 --> 00:16:18,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Do something.

203
00:16:18,613 --> 00:16:24,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, they will disturb that wholesome routine, but will tighten the pressure a bit.

204
00:16:24,701 --> 00:16:26,202
[SPEAKER_01]: And if nothing comes up, great.

205
00:16:26,403 --> 00:16:27,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Tighten it a bit more.

206
00:16:27,644 --> 00:16:29,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing comes up, well, maybe you really finished all the work.

207
00:16:30,187 --> 00:16:32,590
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'm pretty sure things will come up as soon as you start tightening.

208
00:16:36,779 --> 00:16:38,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, as you say, skip a meal.

209
00:16:38,219 --> 00:16:38,839
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to go.

210
00:16:38,859 --> 00:16:50,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when we speak often about, you know, knowing your intentions, you realize, yes, you keep the precepts, you live a wholesome life, day from the morning till the evening, but within that, if you look at your intentions closely, there will be things you do out of distraction.

211
00:16:50,942 --> 00:17:06,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, not kind of seriously unwholesome, sensual desire or ill will and anger, but now we're looking for, like, more refined things, refined blemishes, and there will be things you do out of desire, out of

212
00:17:07,006 --> 00:17:16,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Irritation, out of distraction, not on that big scale, as I said, but still, in its own, it's still the same phenomenon of desire, of ill-will, and that's your work.

213
00:17:16,933 --> 00:17:17,933
[SPEAKER_01]: So, restrain that, then.

214
00:17:21,056 --> 00:17:26,779
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all, you know, restrained by the body, speech, and then the mind, thoughts, intentions, actions by your mind.

215
00:17:26,879 --> 00:17:28,280
[SPEAKER_01]: You start restraining that.

216
00:17:28,761 --> 00:17:35,125
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you're restraining physical actions of, you know, doing things with the body and saying things with the mouth.

217
00:17:42,153 --> 00:17:44,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, fear, fear that you haven't got it all worked out.

218
00:17:45,035 --> 00:17:48,477
[SPEAKER_00]: So then you... everything's calm, but you can have this fear of...

219
00:17:49,158 --> 00:17:51,679
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to have it all worked out, you know, my ideas.

220
00:17:52,120 --> 00:17:55,182
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's again, it would be rooted in that same thing, exactly that thing I said.

221
00:17:55,962 --> 00:18:03,327
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to have things worked out, because things not worked out is too ambiguous and uncomfortable to me.

222
00:18:04,188 --> 00:18:07,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, I want to manage that phenomenon, that discomfort.

223
00:18:09,585 --> 00:18:16,148
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you be sure that you won't disintegrate?

224
00:18:17,428 --> 00:18:24,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, how would I know now that I won't suffer in the future, if all that happens?

225
00:18:24,911 --> 00:18:31,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, as we said in the beginning, come up with specific things that used to make you suffer, that are making you suffer.

226
00:18:32,394 --> 00:18:34,615
[SPEAKER_01]: If that doesn't work, increase the sense restraint.

227
00:18:34,715 --> 00:18:35,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Just see what mind will do.

228
00:18:35,735 --> 00:18:37,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it going to start kicking and screaming?

229
00:18:38,509 --> 00:18:39,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you tighten its harness.

230
00:18:41,290 --> 00:18:42,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, be patient.

231
00:18:42,571 --> 00:18:43,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't slide back.

232
00:18:43,971 --> 00:18:45,772
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, don't go back to centrality and so on.

233
00:18:46,652 --> 00:18:48,973
[SPEAKER_01]: And you wouldn't, if nothing is prompting you.

234
00:18:49,034 --> 00:18:51,655
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't have a reason to go back.

235
00:18:51,755 --> 00:18:54,936
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, be patient for a while and see if something comes up unforeseen.

236
00:18:56,897 --> 00:18:58,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Keeping the precepts of practicing sense.

237
00:18:58,297 --> 00:19:04,000
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why arahants, even themselves, remain mindful and sense restraint, because they prefer now that.

238
00:19:05,816 --> 00:19:09,380
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why often you had, like, even, like, anāgāmīs who thought they were arahants.

239
00:19:09,640 --> 00:19:11,081
[SPEAKER_01]: They couldn't see anything else to be done.

240
00:19:11,942 --> 00:19:13,644
[SPEAKER_01]: Factually, they couldn't see anything else to be done.

241
00:19:13,744 --> 00:19:19,970
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as the Buddha said, there might have been something, some subtle residue, almost, like, virtually invisible residues.

242
00:19:20,010 --> 00:19:21,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So they remain alert.

243
00:19:21,251 --> 00:19:22,533
[SPEAKER_01]: But they remain alert, exactly.

244
00:19:22,593 --> 00:19:23,914
[SPEAKER_01]: So you have nothing to lose either way.

245
00:19:24,214 --> 00:19:26,977
[SPEAKER_01]: If you remain alert, sense restraint, keeping the precepts.

246
00:19:27,157 --> 00:19:28,819
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's also preferred mode of being.

247
00:19:33,244 --> 00:19:34,345
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no end to that, yeah.

248
00:19:34,505 --> 00:19:36,786
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... once you take up that practice.

249
00:19:36,886 --> 00:19:37,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's it.

250
00:19:39,928 --> 00:19:45,291
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if none of those things has... brings up any issues or kilesas or stuff to deal with, fine.

251
00:19:46,292 --> 00:19:54,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you have completed your work, as the Buddha would say, and you would just continue abiding mindful and sense restraint, because that's a pleasant mode of being alive.

252
00:19:54,557 --> 00:19:57,079
[SPEAKER_01]: More... or less unpleasant mode of being alive.

253
00:19:57,219 --> 00:20:00,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Less unpleasant mode of enduring the body and the five aggregates until they break apart.

